<span class="ltx_bibblock ltx_bib_cited">Cited by: <a href="#Sx1.p1.1" title="Introduction ‣ GMoPE: A Prompt-Expert Mixture Framework for Graph Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">Introduction</span></a>.
</span></li>
<li id="bib.bib20" class="ltx_bibitem ltx_bib_inproceedings"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_bib_author-year ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Zhao <span class="ltx_text ltx_bib_etal">et al.</span> (2024)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><span class="ltx_text ltx_bib_author">H. Zhao, A. Chen, X. Sun, H. Cheng, and J. Li</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><span class="ltx_text ltx_bib_title">All in one and one for all: a simple yet effective method towards cross-domain graph pretraining</span>.
</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">In <span class="ltx_text ltx_bib_inbook">Proceedings of the 30th ACM SIGKDD Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining</span>,
</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock ltx_bib_cited">Cited by: <a href="#Sx1.p2.1" title="Introduction ‣ GMoPE: A Prompt-Expert Mixture Framework for Graph Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">Introduction</span></a>.
</span></li>
</ul>
</section>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>A.Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<section id="A1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Theorem Proof</h3>

<div id="A1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we provide a detailed proof and explanation for the Theorem 1.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1 Expressiveness of Concatenation vs. Addition:</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Let <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a node feature and <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> a prompt vector. The concatenation operation <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x;p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x;p]</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly more expressive than the addition operation <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>), in the sense that any function of the form <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x+p^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x+p^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented as <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g([x;p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g([x;p])</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, but not vice versa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lester et al. 2021</a>)</cite>.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.SSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2 (Expressiveness of MoE Routing):</span>
Let <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>single</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the function class realizable by a single expert (e.g., a single MLP or GNN), and let <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MoE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MoE}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the function class realizable by a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) model with <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> experts and a routing function. The MoE model computes</p>
<table id="A1.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathrm{MoE}}(x)=\sum_{j=1}^{M}\alpha_{j}(x)f_{j}(x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathrm{MoE}}(x)=\sum_{j=1}^{M}\alpha_{j}(x)f_{j}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>single</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}(x)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}(x)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum{j=1}^{M}\alpha_{j}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum{j=1}^{M}\alpha_{j}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> determined by the routing function (which may be hard or soft). Then,</p>
<table id="A1.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MoE}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>single</mi></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MoE}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SSx1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SSx1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof:</span>
It is straightforward to see that <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}\subseteq\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MoE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>single</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}\subseteq\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MoE}}</annotation></semantics></math>, since for any <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>single</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can construct an MoE function by setting <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}=f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}=f</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathrm{MoE}}(x)=f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathrm{MoE}}(x)=f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">To show that the inclusion is strict,we exhibit a function <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MoE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MoE}}</annotation></semantics></math> that cannot be represented by any single expert in <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>single</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">Consider the case where <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=2</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},f_{2}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>single</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},f_{2}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}</annotation></semantics></math> be two distinct functions (<math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists x</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(x)\neq f_{2}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(x)\neq f_{2}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>). Define a routing function such that for some non-empty subset <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{1}\subset\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{1}\subset\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input space), <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{2}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{2}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{2}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{2}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}\setminus\mathcal{X}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}\setminus\mathcal{X}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the MoE function is:</p>
<table id="A1.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathrm{MoE}}(x)=\begin{cases}f_{1}(x)&amp;\text{if }x\in\mathcal{X}_{1},\\
f_{2}(x)&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathrm{MoE}}(x)=\begin{cases}f_{1}(x)&amp;\text{if }x\in\mathcal{X}_{1},\\
f_{2}(x)&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SSx1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Assume for contradiction that some <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>single</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=f_{\mathrm{MoE}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=f_{\mathrm{MoE}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. This would require <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to simultaneously match <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> elsewhere, which is impossible when <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>single</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{single}}</annotation></semantics></math> is constrained.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.SSx1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">By Lemma 1 and Lemma 2:</span>
Any function that can be represented by GPF (i.e., addition-based prompt with a single expert) can also be represented by GMoPE (i.e., concatenation-based prompt with MoE), by simply using one expert and setting the prompt accordingly. Therefore,</p>
<table id="A1.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GPF}}\subseteq\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GMoPE}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>GPF</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>GMoPE</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GPF}}\subseteq\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GMoPE}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SSx1.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SSx1.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A concrete example:</span>
Suppose we have two input domains <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., graphs from two different distributions). Assume that that the optimal transformation for <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}([x;p_{1}])=x\odot p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}([x;p_{1}])=x\odot p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{2}([x;p_{2}])=x+p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{2}([x;p_{2}])=x+p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The GMoPE model, with an appropriate routing function, can assign inputs from <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to expert 1 and from <math id="A1.SSx1.p1.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to expert 2, each with its own prompt and GNN parameters. Thus, the overall function is:</p>
<table id="A1.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathrm{GMoPE}}(x)=\alpha_{1}(x)g_{1}([x;p_{1}])+\alpha_{2}(x)g_{2}([x;p_{2}])." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>GMoPE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathrm{GMoPE}}(x)=\alpha_{1}(x)g_{1}([x;p_{1}])+\alpha_{2}(x)g_{2}([x;p_{2}]).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, GPF is limited to applying a single global transformation of the form <math id="A1.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x+p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x+p)</annotation></semantics></math> across all inputs, and thus cannot model domain-specific, piecewise, or convex-combination behaviors. Consequently, the function in question lies within the function space <math id="A1.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GMoPE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>GMoPE</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GMoPE}}</annotation></semantics></math> but not within <math id="A1.SSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GPF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>GPF</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GPF}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">GMoPE activation enables the model to perform group-wise, domain-wise, or even sample-wise feature enhancement, in contrast to the global and uniform transformation applied by GPF. This conditional and adaptive mechanism grants GMoPE fundamentally greater expressive power.
Therefore, we obtain the following strict inclusion:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GPF}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GMoPE}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>GPF</mi></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>GMoPE</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GPF}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{GMoPE}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>B.Experimental Details</h2>

