<section id="A2.SSx1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Citation network</h4>

<div id="A2.SSx1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cora and Citeseer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sen et al. 2008</a>)</cite> jointly provide two small-scale corpora of computer-science literature,
where each node is encoded by a sparse bag-of-words vector and labeled with a corresponding research topic.
Pubmed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Namata et al. 2012</a>)</cite> compiles diabetes-focused abstracts from PubMed; each node is represented by a denser TF-IDF vector and assigned one of three diabetes-type labels.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SSx1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">E-commerce co-purchase graphs</h4>

<div id="A2.SSx1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Computers and Photo are two co-purchase graphs extracted from the Amazon review corpus introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shchur et al. 2018</a>)</cite>. Each node represents a product, and edges indicate that two products are frequently bought together. Node features are TF-IDF vectors derived from customer reviews, and the task is to classify products into sub-categories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SSx1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Molecular graphs</h4>

<div id="A2.SSx1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PROTEINS<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Borgwardt et al. 2005</a>)</cite>, DD<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dobson and Doig 2003</a>)</cite> and NCI109<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wale et al. 2008</a>)</cite> are collections of molecular or protein graphs. Each node represents an atom, and each edge represents a chemical bond. The task is binary graph-level classification: PROTEINS distinguishes enzymes from non-enzymes, DD separates drug-like molecules from decoys, and NCI109 predicts anti-cancer activity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T5" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A2.T5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A2.T5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="A2.T5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="A2.T5.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="A2.T5.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="A2.T5.1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="A2.T5.1.1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Tunning</span></span>
<span id="A2.T5.1.1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="A2.T5.1.1.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Strategy</span></span>
</span></span><span id="A2.T5.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="A2.T5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Cora</td>
